BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 515.6 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0016 | CIRCULATION 1, pp. 52-53) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 492.4 Tm (17. For cardiac output measurement using the oxygen Fick principle, why is blood sampled from the pulmonary) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 478.7 Tm (artery?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 462.0 Tm (Answer: A. It contains mixed venous blood returning from the systemic circulation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 445.7 Tm (Explanation: The pulmonary artery carries a mixture of venous returns from systemic tissues before oxygenation in) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 432.6 Tm (the lungs. A single peripheral vein does not represent the whole-body mixed venous oxygen content.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 416.5 Tm (Other options: A pulmonary artery sample is mixed venous, not systemic arterial blood or a direct stroke-volume) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 403.9 Tm (measurement.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 389.3 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0017 | CIRCULATION 1, pp. 52-53) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 366.1 Tm (18. At the same stroke volume and heart rate, which change generally increases myocardial oxygen demand more?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 349.4 Tm (Answer: C. Pumping against a much higher arterial pressure) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 333.1 Tm (Explanation: Pressure work, which increases with afterload and myocardial wall tension, carries a high oxygen cost.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 320.0 Tm (Pumping the same volume against greater arterial pressure is more demanding.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 303.9 Tm (Other options: The alternative changes reduce pressure load rather than increase it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 289.3 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0018 | CIRCULATION 1, p. 54) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 266.1 Tm (19. According to Poiseuille's law, if a vessel radius is reduced to half while other factors remain constant,) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 252.4 Tm (how does resistance change?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 235.7 Tm (Answer: C. It becomes 16 times greater.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 219.4 Tm (Explanation: Resistance varies inversely with the fourth power of radius. Halving radius therefore increases) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 206.3 Tm (resistance by 2^4 = 16 times.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 190.2 Tm (Other options: The fourth-power dependence is stronger than a linear, square or cubic change.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 175.6 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0019 | CIRCULATION 1, pp. 68-70) Tj ET
q .7 .6 .3 RG .8 w 48 54 m 548 54 l S Q
