BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 602.0 Tm (Answer: E. It increases because blood viscosity increases.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 585.7 Tm (Explanation: A higher red-cell concentration generally increases blood viscosity. Greater viscosity increases) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.6 Tm (resistance to flow under otherwise similar conditions.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 556.5 Tm (Other options: Haematocrit affects blood rheology as well as oxygen carriage.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 541.9 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0021 | CIRCULATION 1, pp. 71-72) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 518.7 Tm (22. Which isolated change increases the tendency for turbulent flow in a blood vessel?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 502.0 Tm (Answer: C. An increase in blood-flow velocity) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 485.7 Tm (Explanation: Reynolds number increases with velocity, density and diameter, and decreases with viscosity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 472.6 Tm (Increased velocity therefore makes turbulence more likely.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 456.5 Tm (Other options: Higher viscosity and smaller diameter generally lower Reynolds number when other variables are held) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 443.9 Tm (constant.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 429.3 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0022 | CIRCULATION 1, pp. 64-66) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 406.1 Tm (23. An electromagnetic blood-flow meter estimates flow by measuring what happens when blood moves through a) Tj ET
