BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 214.3 Tm (Other options: Increased vagal activity would tend to slow the heart rather than compensate for falling pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 199.7 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0035 | CIRCULATION 2, pp. 18-27) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 176.5 Tm (36. Arterial pressure suddenly increases. What happens to the rate of firing of carotid sinus baroreceptors?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 159.8 Tm (Answer: C. It increases because the arterial wall is stretched more.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 143.5 Tm (Explanation: Carotid sinus baroreceptors are stretch-sensitive. Increased arterial pressure increases wall) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 130.4 Tm (distension and afferent firing, contributing to reflex bradycardia and vasodilation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 114.3 Tm (Other options: Baroreceptors respond to pressure-related stretch; carotid bodies are distinct chemoreceptors.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 99.7 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0036 | CIRCULATION 2, pp. 18, 22-24) Tj ET
