BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 618.7 Tm (38. Rapid infusion of fluid increases venous return and stretches the right atrium. Which reflex response may) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.0 Tm (help accommodate the larger venous return?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 588.3 Tm (Answer: B. Heart rate may increase as atrial stretch activates a reflex.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.0 Tm (Explanation: The atrial stretch \(Bainbridge\) reflex can increase heart rate when venous return and atrial filling) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 558.9 Tm (rise. It is distinct from the arterial baroreceptor response to elevated blood pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 542.8 Tm (Other options: Atrial distension can elicit reflex tachycardia without requiring arterial hypotension. The infusion) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.2 Tm (increases rather than reduces venous return.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 515.6 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0038 | CIRCULATION 2, pp. 28-29) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 492.4 Tm (39. Immediately after a person stands up quickly, before reflex compensation has fully developed, what tends) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 478.7 Tm (to happen?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 462.0 Tm (Answer: B. Venous pooling increases and venous return to the heart falls.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 445.7 Tm (Explanation: Gravity increases hydrostatic pressure and pooling in dependent veins. This reduces central venous) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 432.6 Tm (return, initially lowering stroke volume and potentially cerebral perfusion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 416.5 Tm (Other options: Without compensation, standing does not drive leg blood centrally or raise head-level perfusion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 401.9 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0039 | Homeostasis, pp. 2-8) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 378.7 Tm (40. Why can sustained head-to-foot acceleration \(+Gz\) cause a pilot to lose consciousness?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 362.0 Tm (Answer: A. Blood shifts toward the legs, reducing pressure and flow to the brain.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 345.7 Tm (Explanation: Positive G increases the hydrostatic pressure gradient toward the lower body, encouraging dependent) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 332.6 Tm (pooling and reducing cerebral arterial perfusion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 316.5 Tm (Other options: Headward blood shifts and facial congestion are characteristic of negative rather than positive) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 303.9 Tm (head-to-foot G.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 289.3 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0040 | Homeostasis, pp. 9-11) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 266.1 Tm (41. A person loses a substantial amount of blood. What sequence most directly explains the initial fall in) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 252.4 Tm (cardiac output?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 235.7 Tm (Answer: D. Lower blood volume leads to lower venous return and ventricular filling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 219.4 Tm (Explanation: Blood loss reduces circulating volume and venous return, which reduces preload and stroke volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 206.3 Tm (Reflex vasoconstriction may defend pressure but cannot automatically restore lost flow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 190.2 Tm (Other options: Loss of circulating volume does not directly raise preload or stroke volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 175.6 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0041 | Homeostasis, pp. 12-20) Tj ET
