BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 458.3 Tm (Explanation: Cerebral perfusion pressure is approximately MAP - ICP when ICP is the effective downstream) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 445.2 Tm (pressure: 95 - 15 = 80 mmHg.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 429.1 Tm (Other options: Perfusion pressure is a difference between driving and downstream pressures; it is not the MAP alone.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 414.5 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0049 | Regional Circulation, pp. 21-23) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.3 Tm (50. Why does most left ventricular coronary blood flow occur during diastole rather than systole?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 374.6 Tm (Answer: E. Contracting left ventricular muscle compresses vessels within its wall.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 358.3 Tm (Explanation: During left ventricular systole, high intramyocardial pressure compresses coronary vessels,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 345.2 Tm (especially in the subendocardium. Relaxation during diastole permits better perfusion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 329.1 Tm (Other options: Coronary arterial openings remain connected to the aorta; the critical factor is compression by contracting) Tj ET
