BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 631.3 Tm (53. During exercise, pulmonary blood flow increases greatly but pulmonary arterial pressure rises relatively) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 617.6 Tm (little. What helps explain this?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 600.9 Tm (Answer: B. Previously underperfused capillaries open and existing vessels widen.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 584.6 Tm (Explanation: Recruitment of additional pulmonary capillaries and distension of vessels increase the total flow) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 571.5 Tm (capacity, limiting the rise in pulmonary vascular resistance and pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 555.4 Tm (Other options: Recruitment and distension expand, not reduce, pulmonary vascular capacity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 540.8 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0053 | Regional Circulation, pp. 30-32 \(concept extension\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 517.6 Tm (54. During severe haemorrhage, sympathetic vasoconstriction reduces blood flow to the gut. What is one) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.9 Tm (immediate benefit of this response?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 487.2 Tm (Answer: B. It helps preserve perfusion pressure for vital organs by reducing splanchnic flow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 470.9 Tm (Explanation: The splanchnic bed is a large vascular reservoir. Sympathetic constriction can redistribute blood and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 457.8 Tm (support systemic pressure during shock, although prolonged gut ischemia can be harmful.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 441.7 Tm (Other options: Reduced splanchnic flow supports central circulation but does not guarantee normal cardiac output or) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 429.1 Tm (prevent gut ischemia.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 414.5 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0054 | Regional Circulation, pp. 34-36) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.3 Tm (55. After firm stroking of the skin, redness spreads beyond the directly stimulated line. What produces this) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.6 Tm (surrounding flare?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 360.9 Tm (Answer: A. An axon reflex causing nearby arterioles to dilate) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 344.6 Tm (Explanation: The flare of the triple response is produced by an antidromic axon reflex that dilates vessels around) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 331.5 Tm (the stimulated region.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 315.4 Tm (Other options: The wheal reflects increased local permeability, whereas the flare is spreading arteriolar vasodilation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 300.8 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0055 | Regional Circulation, pp. 39-40) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 277.6 Tm (56. Fetal haemoglobin binds oxygen more strongly than adult haemoglobin under comparable conditions. Which) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 263.9 Tm (property contributes to this difference?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 247.2 Tm (Answer: E. Fetal haemoglobin binds 2,3-BPG less strongly.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 230.9 Tm (Explanation: The lower affinity of fetal haemoglobin for 2,3-BPG contributes to its higher oxygen affinity,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 217.8 Tm (supporting oxygen transfer across the placenta.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 201.7 Tm (Other options: Fetal haemoglobin still unloads oxygen to tissues; it simply has a left-shifted dissociation curve compared) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 189.1 Tm (with adult haemoglobin.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 174.5 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0056 | Regional Circulation, p. 45) Tj ET
