BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 01 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (57. What change occurs in the pulmonary circulation with the newborn's first breaths?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 728.3 Tm (Answer: C. Pulmonary vascular resistance falls as the lungs expand and oxygen rises.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 712.0 Tm (Explanation: Lung expansion and increased alveolar oxygen reduce pulmonary vascular resistance, raising pulmonary) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.9 Tm (blood flow. Loss of the placenta simultaneously tends to raise systemic vascular resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 682.8 Tm (Other options: The first breaths promote pulmonary vasodilation, not vasoconstriction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 668.2 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0057 | Regional Circulation, pp. 42-45 \(concept extension\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 645.0 Tm (58. Which pair of electrodes forms standard bipolar lead I in an ECG?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.3 Tm (Answer: D. Right arm \(negative\) and left arm \(positive\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 612.0 Tm (Explanation: Standard lead I measures the potential difference LA\(+\) minus RA\(-\). Lead II uses RA\(-\) and LL\(+\),) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 598.9 Tm (while lead III uses LA\(-\) and LL\(+\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 582.8 Tm (Other options: Lead II and III use the left-leg electrode; reversing the lead I polarity is also incorrect.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 568.2 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0058 | ECG 2, pp. 3-5) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 545.0 Tm (59. An ECG has a PR interval of 0.24 seconds, with a normal-width QRS complex and every P wave followed by a) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 531.3 Tm (QRS. What does this pattern indicate?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 514.6 Tm (Answer: A. First-degree AV block \(prolonged AV conduction\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 498.3 Tm (Explanation: A PR interval longer than 0.20 s with one-to-one AV conduction is first-degree AV block. The) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 485.2 Tm (normal-width QRS is compatible with delayed AV conduction without loss of beats.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 469.1 Tm (Other options: Complete AV block requires loss of consistent P-to-QRS conduction; atrial fibrillation has no regular) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 456.5 Tm (organised P waves.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 441.9 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0059 | ECG 2, pp. 6-8) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 418.7 Tm (60. In the ECG strip, the distance between consecutive R waves is 20 small squares. The paper speed is 25) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 405.0 Tm (mm/s. What is the heart rate?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 388.3 Tm (Answer: B. 75 beats/min) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 372.0 Tm (Explanation: At 25 mm/s, one small square = 0.04 s. Twenty squares = 0.8 s per beat, giving 60/0.8 = 75 beats/min.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 358.9 Tm (Equivalently, 1500/20 = 75 beats/min.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 342.8 Tm (Other options: Heart rate depends on the R-R interval; the standard 25 mm/s conversion yields 1500 divided by small) Tj ET
